Gaussian Elimination using Partial or Complete Pivoting
• Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting:
▶ Search the element with the largest absolute value in the first column ▶ Interchange this row with the first row using elementary transformation ▶ Carry out the elimination of the first column ▶ Search for the largest element in the second column of K1A, excluding first element and bring to a1
22 position ▶ Process is more stable as |eij | ≤ 1
• Gaussian elimination with complete pivoting:
▶ Use of both elementary row and column operations ▶ Search the element with the largest absolute value among all elements ▶ Move the element to the first column by the elementary column operation and then to the first row by elementary row operation ▶ Carry out the elimination of the first column ▶ Repeat the process with the remaining matrix ▶ Process is more stable than partial pivoting, but at the cost of time-consuming processing
• Partial pivoting more popular w.r.t. overall performance (efficiency, accuracy, reliability, generality, ease of use, etc)
