Solution of Linear Equations
Consider the matrix equation Ax = y, A : (F n, F ) → (F m, F ).
• Given A and y in (F m, F ), there exists a vector x such that Ax = y iff the vector y is an element of R(A), ρ(A) = ρ([A : y]).
• Given A, for every y in (F m, F ), there exists a vector x such that Ax = y iff R(A) = (F m, F ) or equivalently, ρ(A) = m.
• Follows from the definition of range space.
• If y ∈/ R(A), Ax = y has no solution and is said to be inconsistent.
• ρ(A): Dimension of R(A)
• Since R(A) is subspace of (F m, F ), ρ(A) = m =⇒ R(A) = (F m, F ).
• If R(A) = (F m, F ), then for every y ∈ (F m, F ), ∃ x s.t. Ax = y.
• If ρ(A) < m, ∃ at least one nonzero vector y ∈ (F m, F ), but y ∈/ R(A) for which there exist no x s.t. Ax = y.
