Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors
Let A be a linear operator that maps (Cn, C) into itself. A scalar λ ∈ C is called an eigenvalue of A if there exists a nonzero vector x s.t, Ax = λx. Any nonzero x satisfying Ax = λx is called an eigenvector of A, associated with the eigenvalue λ.
Ax = λx =⇒ (A − λI) x = 0
• For a nontrivial solution, det(A − λI) = 0.
• λ is an eigenvalue iff it is a solution of the characteristic polynomial
∆(λ) ≜ det(A − λI) = 0
• Number of eigenvalues same as the degree, n, of ∆(λ)
• Eigenvalues are not necessarily distinct.
• Right and left eigenvectors: Ax = λx, yA = λy
